#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/id #uuid "121d5f8c-3b38-5c75-aec1-646dc7d2f2f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/id #uuid "9e531872-846f-5e75-97c0-dd2db3515558" :question/math-academy-id "e-14721" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "d38ac709-38e8-51a1-8e08-14913c77639c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14722" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c556d14b-70e2-5ca2-a713-25a69f146954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295789} {:answer/id #uuid "45bd1e36-3565-5059-9978-17a29caaedef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295790} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d66724-67af-5fbb-b742-1db1094234b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295791} {:answer/id #uuid "7e593f1f-62bc-5168-932e-59309def1365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295792} {:answer/id #uuid "2aedf132-d24d-5212-9f42-ff8047e1aa2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aedf132-d24d-5212-9f42-ff8047e1aa2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295793} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6d83d3-c1bc-5852-905d-98bc3e78e569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c30c5f22-36f0-5965-9fed-d4b105565e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-110338" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+10}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ x+10 & =4x+4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+10}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{9}{{(2+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3003d2c6-3b38-5e95-b719-5b9a0d5c9642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283000} {:answer/id #uuid "02ed77c0-f3e4-541a-8b4b-30cb5e3a1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283001} {:answer/id #uuid "dab393c9-b76c-5660-bb79-30c2d41d6439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283002} {:answer/id #uuid "e84b7c88-92b9-57ba-a71c-0fc867044a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283003} {:answer/id #uuid "58eefba0-9a07-54df-9842-45ae83596040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02ed77c0-f3e4-541a-8b4b-30cb5e3a1ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283001} :answer-field/id #uuid "746eb990-d795-58ac-b486-d547ad6ba17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9233943-9639-5785-9a46-de416a7119f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15641" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=9-\\frac{2}{3}x$ and the point $P(3,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =9-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acd009f3-ae84-5bf6-9c44-e7fcdf8b3a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295796} {:answer/id #uuid "13cb97fe-9b74-58cd-89b8-6a209d64a358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295797} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc94adc-ca4b-504d-8858-062876eb3fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295798} {:answer/id #uuid "62c45b8f-4bd1-5b10-9494-818e9cc1998a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295799} {:answer/id #uuid "069c5138-e7b7-5507-822e-4670dae7e2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acd009f3-ae84-5bf6-9c44-e7fcdf8b3a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295796} :answer-field/id #uuid "48dfbb50-946c-5981-abd7-1282378dd376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba779119-012f-5aba-bccf-539b483e38de" :question/math-academy-id "q-15709" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/0d7c1b55cbc73a769f7ab0c0c8e634d54356eb36e77dc7ca0d06ff77d9367162.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(2,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(2,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73bf5e7b-fa30-5a4d-a081-82a6905392ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295803} {:answer/id #uuid "6edc8b38-4fb2-5970-b364-9f9c67e2e9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295804} {:answer/id #uuid "995de621-ea4f-5be3-9bfa-c3bea3b38f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295805} {:answer/id #uuid "37916253-c11b-5258-9880-f77aade288fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295806} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9afa23-7f21-5bfd-99fe-558a3d95a546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "995de621-ea4f-5be3-9bfa-c3bea3b38f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295805} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9b5459-d3ea-5931-864a-91d802f0272f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e2a8e81-0143-5ac3-9218-1837a0d8d00a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18601" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/7e881b5cb3613ff72264739b1cbe1214c1041a78d201cfc323476910e7ce8e3c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(4,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,4)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(4)=1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} {:answer/id #uuid "0f65b70c-4513-5d7f-88de-bf7e421f1942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} :answer-field/id #uuid "40350093-241a-55dd-ac9f-9a83f78e707e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c43205-3a68-5865-9baf-3488c8c39bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-312578" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c252298-e973-55f9-b09e-7bb41ab18e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295814} {:answer/id #uuid "99ad25f2-831a-51b2-bf5b-1d70cd523db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c252298-e973-55f9-b09e-7bb41ab18e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295814} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ba0f3b-3db3-5e26-bb8f-963cf1a8bd6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9315fbdb-8a10-5769-ba1d-a74c3c6c47b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-312580" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+{2}^{x}$ and the point $P(0,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+{2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x+{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295818} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd9d412-937f-5c3d-b8be-8ecf613b4921" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1490c31-6761-5f3f-8b46-c37dbd062af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-312583" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1f55afc-34aa-53e1-8394-f61ac6801b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f55afc-34aa-53e1-8394-f61ac6801b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295821} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b00cba8-1263-5ce0-b5e1-148424743a6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cdbca75-994b-53ca-b31e-22e3ae38d84f" :question/math-academy-id "q-312585" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(8)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-4x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4({2}^{3})-4(2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295824} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e16358-519e-5455-b910-148e3f8eb26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "422cbd5e-38e5-544a-b056-53061bf5421a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342562" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95051f6c-2cda-512b-a166-85bf75a4fb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95051f6c-2cda-512b-a166-85bf75a4fb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295827} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8d23cac-45b4-5c6c-8c7d-2aa0b595e105" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d0c5039-62c4-5869-afca-b3d561b286f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342570" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/feb02396a635c3f416b07383b03269e12fde738da3345aa6d6d9fe5350745746.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0.5)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0.5,1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(0.5,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0.5)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0.5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0c5b6b-e340-5575-8040-e67e50780fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279596} {:answer/id #uuid "38f06589-d04c-547d-a8f1-0ce386c86214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279597} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac894c4-80f6-5d55-8c9a-7c86cba29ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f06589-d04c-547d-a8f1-0ce386c86214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279597} :answer-field/id #uuid "32abd2ef-a001-5271-8715-9a083d0a399d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1feeaa24-053b-5b18-a92c-a34f2428102b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342579" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and the point $P(1,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635ae1bd-f5f8-56de-b9e5-7ff6a429bfb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "635ae1bd-f5f8-56de-b9e5-7ff6a429bfb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295831} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ce9f09-7db6-5f63-94a1-ce00fb0d1697" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8afa493-ae6a-5bc8-a39b-9bb74c7ad881" :question/math-academy-id "q-342582" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295834} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee9e45-6561-505c-b53a-1631b71fe964" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "693dd3d0-3454-5894-a36e-0df2728d3681" :question/math-academy-id "q-342583" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d35ad3ef-28f1-557d-bbec-6ef6c4696fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283014} {:answer/id #uuid "23122002-68de-55a3-8698-55d189a802c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d35ad3ef-28f1-557d-bbec-6ef6c4696fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283014} :answer-field/id #uuid "071430b0-ac1c-5c5d-bfb0-7aacd0d96541" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec34106c-1275-5721-882b-cb32e2aa950e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342589" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ff3980-1fed-50f1-9128-e3ca6632b8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279606} {:answer/id #uuid "bff14158-2799-57d3-9c92-9753e0e65652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ff3980-1fed-50f1-9128-e3ca6632b8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279606} :answer-field/id #uuid "630d3a62-d1f0-5ad8-b11f-df4ca1ba102f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b938f4e-7aed-514c-aa88-1f8816849a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-342596" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f224817-f1c2-52b8-a838-c6627808bcbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295838} {:answer/id #uuid "571a121e-249d-519e-92f9-1f1502bde7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f224817-f1c2-52b8-a838-c6627808bcbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295838} :answer-field/id #uuid "03616ae6-f9da-5572-920b-0d866b07f62d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08e8f5ed-5b1e-519e-b4c4-4b136ca13f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-342599" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-x.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\le -1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\le -1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ccffb9-54f8-5c25-af55-6ee070b8e6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295841} {:answer/id #uuid "41dd22ae-0f48-5cae-b59e-79055a39b45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295842} {:answer/id #uuid "5711550e-95e7-5129-bb9a-e701d205d319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295843} {:answer/id #uuid "7b652385-36d1-5d58-ac68-b26d0a5efd5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295844} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7771e5-051c-5e6f-9ab7-ba649a397c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ccffb9-54f8-5c25-af55-6ee070b8e6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295841} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8ef53e-f61c-513a-88fd-13b41b37c397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fbff772-e6e5-5110-9b66-ccc497ff0d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-75829" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=5$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2b4e94b-b9a9-5a3e-8b0f-bcfffccaf066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295848} {:answer/id #uuid "000d7775-0402-5005-bbb1-a7a52ebb2cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295849} {:answer/id #uuid "93b248f2-0ad6-566c-9f25-1c175397f527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295850} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1a8b23-aa69-5471-bb76-b991d477d1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295851} {:answer/id #uuid "b461f741-12b5-584c-8580-dc84fca2ab6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "000d7775-0402-5005-bbb1-a7a52ebb2cb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295849} :answer-field/id #uuid "f574523f-5ae6-5179-87fe-870299a88403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38a623ea-a52e-5dd0-8781-88f72b4e9cf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75831" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=-6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964b338c-ec7b-55f0-8ec9-26e61ddc1a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063700} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa2b59f-1dd2-5c21-9a9f-2e6766820f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063701} {:answer/id #uuid "55a4f157-fee3-5ef6-8e0a-7b24a9c266c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063702} {:answer/id #uuid "7185db66-1354-58fd-ba7e-b77360446aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063703} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4e47e2-72d9-53e1-a30a-690757f708b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7185db66-1354-58fd-ba7e-b77360446aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063703} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e2bc766-7d15-5dff-9fa2-341eea84dc49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a674864-526f-5399-9fef-530d9892c47d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76114" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59821fc8-e9df-5397-8f51-eb936149f62d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295855} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d66dab-93a0-5e57-b08d-c5fef1224017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295856} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e7b9cc-271b-5641-9a48-5cb756f4d2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295857} {:answer/id #uuid "3bea40ac-6a9a-5644-8b15-d06af405881c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295858} {:answer/id #uuid "35a03d42-f1c5-5c7f-ba9d-b0db949ac390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e7b9cc-271b-5641-9a48-5cb756f4d2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295857} :answer-field/id #uuid "521de4c2-d1f7-5a30-9d43-b3d0a36305f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8be70d73-596d-5442-ba07-c1e2659c3b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76128" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=1+2{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ 1+2{e}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(3)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =1+2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
